# Trading core quality

## Smoke checks (from `apps/api`)

```bash
pip install -r requirements.txt
python -c "from app.main import app"
```

## Runtime

- `GET /health` — process up.
- `GET /eod/status`, `POST /eod/master/refresh-if-due` — per `RUNBOOK.md`.
- For recurring status-style endpoints such as freshness, prefer precomputed or cached summary state over repeated full-table aggregate reads when the source tables are large.

## Performance and indexing guardrails

- Treat date-driven reads on large tables as index-sensitive by default.
- When adding or changing queries that depend on recentness or latest-state semantics, explicitly review index coverage for columns such as `market_date`, `trade_date`, `receipt_date`, and common FK-plus-date access patterns.
- For large append-heavy tables, consider indexes during schema design rather than waiting for production regressions.
- When performance changes after storage moves, cache resets, or dataset growth, run `python3.11 -m app.internal.index_audit` from `apps/api` before guessing.
- Use the audit output plus `pg_stat_user_tables`, `pg_indexes`, and, when needed, `pg_stat_progress_create_index` to identify hot sequential scans and confirm index work completion.
- If an endpoint only needs the latest known status, consider a summary/cache/materialized-state write path instead of recomputing from large historical tables on every request.

## Documentation

- Keep **`docs/API_BUILD_ORDER.md`** in sync with implemented routes.
- When behavior affects the Next.js BFF, note alignment with the consumer’s API contract.

## CI

If `.github/workflows/ci.yml` is present, it should install requirements under `apps/api` and run the import smoke command. Add pytest later under `apps/api/tests/` when the suite exists.
